# thermal.conf - site config for the GPU/CPU-load-driven fan controller.
#
# Copy to ~/.config/smcfan/thermal.conf (or point THERMAL_CONF at another
# path) and edit the values you need. Every key below is optional: a missing
# key keeps controller.sh's built-in default, and a missing file entirely
# runs on defaults with the internal fans only. Lines starting with # and
# blank lines are ignored; unknown keys are reported to stderr and ignored,
# never silently accepted. This file is parsed line by line, never sourced -
# only the keys listed here can ever be set from it.

# --- GPU signal --------------------------------------------------------
# Utilisation percent (from `ioreg -r -c IOGPU -d 1 -f`, "Device Utilization
# %") above which the GPU is considered hot. Desktop use alone can read well
# into the 40-70% range on some machines - measure your own idle baseline
# first (controller.sh probe) and set this comfortably above it.
GPU_UTIL_PCT=85

# --- CPU signal ----------------------------------------------------------
# CPU power in mW (from the powermetrics sidecar log) above which the CPU is
# considered hot. Drives the internal ramp only, never an external fan.
CPU_LOAD_MW=8000

# --- timing ----------------------------------------------------------------
POLL_INTERVAL=15            # seconds between ticks in `loop` mode
ON_SUSTAIN_SECONDS=60        # CPU must stay hot this long before the ramp engages
OFF_SUSTAIN_SECONDS=30       # either signal must stay cool this long before releasing
ALERT_AFTER_SECONDS=120      # GPU below threshold this long after being above -> one alert
ALERT_CLEAR_SECONDS=120      # GPU back above threshold this long before the alert latch re-arms

# --- internal fan curve (smcfand) -------------------------------------------
SMCFAN_SENSOR=cpu_core_average   # sensor smcfand ramps against
SMCFAN_MIN_C=45                  # curve floor: min RPM below this temperature
SMCFAN_MAX_C=75                  # curve ceiling: max RPM at/above this temperature
SMCFAN_SMOOTH_S=20               # EMA time constant (s) smoothing the ramp's temperature input;
                                  # cuts the power-gated-core noise that otherwise swings the fan
                                  # target ~1500 RPM every 2s poll. 0 disables smoothing.
# SMCFAN_CTL=/path/to/smcfan-ctl # override; defaults to ../Scripts/smcfan-ctl next to controller.sh

# --- powermetrics sidecar log ------------------------------------------
# PRESSURE_LOG=/tmp/t1-powermetrics-smc.log

# --- optional external fan --------------------------------------------------
# Any shell command, run with `bash -c`, no arguments. Leave all three unset
# to run internal-fans-only - the whole external-fan section of every tick is
# then skipped outright, not merely a no-op.
#   EXTERNAL_ON_CMD      turns the external fan on;  must exit 0 on success
#   EXTERNAL_OFF_CMD     turns the external fan off;  must exit 0 on success
#   EXTERNAL_STATUS_CMD  prints exactly "on", "off", or "unknown" (optional -
#                        it is a manual-diagnostics hook only, used by
#                        `controller.sh probe` and `reset`. The tick loop
#                        never calls it: it decides purely from what it last
#                        commanded, so a real switch (e.g. a Home Assistant
#                        entity) is never polled between threshold crossings.
#                        A side effect: a human who flips the switch by hand
#                        is not detected or fought - it sticks until the next
#                        real crossing.)
#
# EXTERNAL_ON_CMD=/path/to/my-fan-on.sh
# EXTERNAL_OFF_CMD=/path/to/my-fan-off.sh
# EXTERNAL_STATUS_CMD=/path/to/my-fan-status.sh

# --- optional cool-dip alert hook -------------------------------------------
# Run once when the GPU drops back below GPU_UTIL_PCT after a sustained hot
# episode (see ALERT_AFTER_SECONDS/ALERT_CLEAR_SECONDS above). The event's
# numbers are passed as env vars: THERMAL_ALERT_COOL_SECONDS,
# THERMAL_ALERT_GPU_UTIL_PCT, THERMAL_ALERT_FAN, THERMAL_ALERT_HOST. Empty =
# no hook call, but the log line and the sentinel file are still written.
#
# ALERT_CMD=/path/to/my-alert.sh
